<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mali Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mali empires</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mali Empire was a West African empire founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> around 1235, which flourished until the fifteenth century. It controlled trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt and reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, whose pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324 became famous. It succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, and its decline was followed by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mali-empire.jpg" alt="Mali Empire" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Mali_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mali Empire</b>, which absorbed Wagadou in the thirteenth century, replicated and expanded Ghana's trans-Saharan commercial architecture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam was introduced through traders: the Kano Chronicle credits Wangarawa merchants from the <b>Mali Empire</b> with bringing it to Kano under Sarki Yaji in the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire arose on the western margins of the <b>Mali Empire</b>, whose hold over the far west of the Sahel was loosening.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kaabu Empire emerged in the thirteenth century as a western province of the <b>Mali Empire</b>, in the region corresponding to present-day Guinea-Bissau, southern Senegal and The Gambia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam reached the court under Yaji (1349–1385), when Wangara Muslims from the <b>Mali Empire</b> arrived and supplied an imam and a judge, but it spread gradually, and many rural Hausa, the Maguzawa, kept the old religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its roots, however, extend much further: Songhai-speaking peoples had occupied the bend of the Niger River around Gao since at least the ninth century CE, and a Songhai kingdom under the Za dynasty (or Dia dynasty) had for a time been a tributary of the <b>Mali Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
